The LoopDriver event-loop thread must not throw. lely's ev::Loop::run() and
poll() throwing wrappers read the thread-local error context after the loop
returns, so an already-handled failure (an SDO abort during a slave boot, a
failed fopen) left there surfaces as a std::system_error out of the thread
function when the loop is stopped for teardown, and the process dies in
std::terminate. Seen on a two-node Panther as "terminate called after
throwing an instance of 'std::system_error', what(): run: No such file or
directory" whenever a boot failure or a container stop tore the drivers
down. Switch Impl_::Start() to the non-throwing overloads.

diff --git a/src/coapp/loop_driver.cpp b/src/coapp/loop_driver.cpp
index 021ad27..a11600a 100644
--- a/src/coapp/loop_driver.cpp
+++ b/src/coapp/loop_driver.cpp
@@ -151,9 +151,14 @@ LoopDriver::Impl_::Start() {
   auto exec = self->GetExecutor();

   // Start the event loop. Signal the existence of a fake task to prevent the
-  // loop for stopping early.
+  // loop for stopping early. Use the non-throwing overloads of run() and
+  // poll(): the throwing wrappers report the thread-local error left by the
+  // last failed operation even if that failure was already handled and the
+  // loop stopped normally, and an exception escaping this thread function
+  // terminates the process.
   exec.on_task_init();
-  loop.run();
+  ::std::error_code ec;
+  loop.run(ec);
   exec.on_task_fini();

   // Deregister the driver to prevent the master from queueing new events. This
@@ -162,7 +167,7 @@ LoopDriver::Impl_::Start() {

   // Finish remaining tasks, but do not block.
   loop.restart();
-  loop.poll();
+  loop.poll(ec);

   // Satisfy the promise to signal that the thread is about to terminate and it
   // a call to the destructor will not block.
